'''water-hickory''' (wâ′tėr-hik″ọ̄-ri), ''n.'' Same as ''bitter pecan'' (which see, under ''pecan'').

'''water-hoarhound''' (wâ′tėr-hōr″hound), ''n.'' A plant of the genus ''Lycopus'', chiefly ''L. Europæus''.

'''water-hog''' (wâ′tėr-hog), ''n.'' '''1.''' The African river-hog, ''Potamochœrus penicillatus''. See cut under ''Potamochœrus''.—'''2.''' The South American capibara, ''Hydrochœrus capibara''. Also called ''tailless hippopotamus'' and ''short-nosed tapir''.

'''water-hole''' (wâ′tėr-hōl), ''n.'' A hole or hollow where water collects. {{smaller|In Australia, a small natural or artificial reservoir; in South Africa, a natural pool, or water-pool. This word is chiefly used in Australia, where it means a small pond or pool of water, and especially such as are filled during the rainy season and dry up when that ceases, or soon after.}}
{{smaller block|In the dry weather, as the small lagoons and ''water-holes'' scattered all over the country [Australia] get low and dried up, large numbers of . . . wild ducks congregate on the big lagoon in front of Mount Spencer station.
{{right|''H. F. Hatton'', Advance Australia, p. 88.}}}}
{{smaller block|We have been drafting close here up at the one-eyed ''waterhole''.
{{right|''Mrs. Campbell Praed'', The Head-Station, p. 84.}}}}

'''waterhole''' (wâ′tėr-hōl), ''v. i.''; pret. and pp. ''waterholed'', ppr. ''waterholing''. [< ''water-hole'', ''n.''] In ''coffee-cultivation''. See the quotation.

readings.c1s3:
